  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B040",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For developing optical lattice clocks that enable precision tests of fundamental physics."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B026",
    "B002"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain optical lattice clocks as my preferred achievement. Their foundational advance is the combination of laser-cooled atoms trapped in an optical lattice with precision spectroscopy, which suppresses key sources of clock uncertainty and makes optical frequency comparisons reproducible across laboratories. The case is not simply that clocks keep improving: this established method has already enabled measurements of gravitational redshift and tests of fundamental physics. Katori and Ye represent complementary experimental approaches and a clear, focused attribution.",
    "case_against": "The continuing gains in clock performance make it necessary to distinguish the foundational method from later refinements. A prize should not imply that every application or subsequent improvement belongs to the original achievement. The central historical question is whether the decisive contributions represented by Katori and Ye are sufficiently distinct and well documented.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that atom interferometry turns matter-wave phase into a sensitive probe of inertial forces and fundamental interactions, with repeated demonstrations supporting maturity; she notes that the long development history complicates attribution.",
        "response": "I agree that repeated demonstrations establish atom interferometry as a mature method, and its reach into precision measurement is compelling. The long sequence of theoretical and experimental contributions makes it harder to identify a compact foundational achievement and exact credit. Optical lattice clocks also serve as measurement instruments, but the trapping and spectroscopy method provides a more bounded experimental advance, with Katori and Ye's complementary approaches supporting the proposed attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that circuit QED merits recognition for establishing controllable coherent interactions between microwave photons and engineered atoms, while noting that individual credit and the boundary with later engineering and the 2025 prize need care.",
        "response": "That is a strong case for a foundational quantum-optics achievement, and I agree that the citation must remain distinct from later engineering. The same boundary question applies to clocks: the relevant achievement is the optical lattice clock method, not the entire continuing precision program. I favor B040 because its experimental advance and the physical measurements it has already enabled can be stated directly, without claiming that future performance gains are part of the prize."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Historical review should establish the decisive contributions of Katori and Ye to the optical lattice clock method and how their approaches relate.",
      "The committee must distinguish the mature clock method and demonstrated fundamental tests from continuing improvements in precision and later applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My research background and WACQT leadership give me professional proximity to quantum technology broadly. The supplied profile identifies no direct collaboration or institutional connection with Katori or Ye; I disclose this proximity for consideration."
  }
}
